en you have  one job and only one and that is support  all you have to do is support you have  to be there for those people you don't  necessarily have to say all that much  your physical presence means a lot you  just you can offer things to them you  can try to think of practical ways that  might help them like cooking a meal all  those kinds of things that's all you do  to the people who are who are closer  into the center support that's all you  do now maybe you feel very grieved by  this situation yourself maybe you feel  like you know this person they're great  friends to feel like that  but your burdens are not meant to go to  people on the inside those people are  feeling more of a burden and more grief  than you if you have burdens and grief  and troubles which you ought to have you  should push those two people outside and  you should say the people who know them  know the person less well than you you  know I'm just finding this really hard  I'm exhausted and I I don't even know  how to cope at work now that this person  is gone or etc all of those are griefs  and burdens and difficulties they have a  place but that place is not going back  onto the people who are closer into the  situation they should be going to people  further out and stories you know for you  you know are one of the most common  sorts of reactions to stories of  difficulty in trial is we think of our  own difficulty and trial and so you know  if you have if you want to make  comparisons to your own situation you  don't do that to the people inside you  know closer to the center of the circle  because they're just feeling the grief  and struggling to come to terms with it  themselves let alone dealing with you  know you and your own problems which are  something altogether different so if you  want to talk about those talk to people  outside further out from the circle and  that'll be a way that you make sure you  don't say things that are inappropriate  or unhelpful to people if you l